MAXIMUM SUM INCREASING SUBSEQUENCE (MSIS)

🔥 Very important

Asked when interviewer wants more than LIS

Tests DP thinking beyond length → value-based DP

Common in product-based & service-based company rounds

📌 Problem Statement

Given an array of integers arr[], find the maximum possible sum of an increasing subsequence.

📌 Conditions:

Subsequence must be strictly increasing

Elements need not be contiguous

✍ Example
Input:  arr = {1, 101, 2, 3, 100}
Output: 106


Explanation:
The maximum sum increasing subsequence is:

1 → 2 → 3 → 100
Sum = 106

🧠 Key Difference from LIS
LIS	MSIS
Maximizes length	Maximizes sum
DP based on count	DP based on values
Result = length	Result = sum
📐 DP Definition

Let

dp[i] = maximum sum of increasing subsequence ending at index i

🧮 Algorithm (Step-by-Step)

Initialize dp[i] = arr[i]
(Each element is a subsequence of length 1)

For every i from 1 to n-1

For every j from 0 to i-1

If arr[j] < arr[i]:

dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + arr[i])


Answer = max(dp[i]) for all i

💻 Java Code (Interview-ready)
public class MaximumSumIncreasingSubsequence {

    public static int maxSumIS(int[] arr) {

        int n = arr.length;
        int[] dp = new int[n];

        // Step 1: Initialize
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            dp[i] = arr[i];
        }

        // Step 2: Build DP
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {

                if (arr[j] < arr[i]) {
                    dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + arr[i]);
                }
            }
        }

        // Step 3: Find max sum
        int maxSum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, dp[i]);
        }

        return maxSum;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 101, 2, 3, 100};
        System.out.println(maxSumIS(arr));
    }
}

🔍 Line-by-Line Explanation
int[] dp = new int[n];


→ dp[i] stores maximum sum ending at index i

dp[i] = arr[i];


→ Single element subsequence → sum is element itself

for (int i = 1; i < n; i++)


→ Consider each element as a possible ending element

for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)


→ Check all previous elements

if (arr[j] < arr[i])


→ Ensure strictly increasing

dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i], dp[j] + arr[i]);


→ Either:

start new subsequence at i, or

extend subsequence ending at j

maxSum = Math.max(maxSum, dp[i]);


→ Final answer is maximum over all DP states

🧪 Dry Run
arr = {1, 101, 2, 3, 100}

dp[] progression:
i=0 → [1]
i=1 → [1, 102]
i=2 → [1,102,3]
i=3 → [1,102,3,6]
i=4 → [1,102,3,6,106]


✅ Answer = 106

⏱ Complexity
Type	Complexity
Time	O(n²)
Space	O(n)
🎯 Interview Talking Points

“This is a value-based DP variation of LIS”

Initialization is crucial

Can’t use binary search optimization like LIS

Asked as a follow-up to LIS
